module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.CharacterLineCohomology
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.SimpleModuleDualHighestWeight
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.RhoSectionModules
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.ReductiveBorelPairStructure
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.RhoRegularWeylOrbit

/-! # Simple images from top flag-line cohomology to global sections

Source: Andersen, The strong linkage principle (1980), pp.55 and 58, Lemma 4;
Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), 22.17--22.30 and the contragredient paragraph, p.471.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-top-bottom-cohomology-simple-image)

The two Borels determine opposite positive systems. Characters are actual torus
characters written additively; e represents the longest Weyl element by normalizer
conjugation. The two line bundles and their cohomology actions are the normalized
associated-character constructions. A simple submodule is expressed as a nonzero
stable subspace minimal among nonzero stable subspaces. Its highest line is its
intersection with the positive unipotent-radical invariants of the ambient module.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section
set_option autoImplicit false
universe u
open CategoryTheory MonoidalCategory CartesianMonoidalCategory
open scoped MonObj TensorProduct
namespace AlgebraicGeometry.RationalHighestWeight
open CharacterLineCohomology

/-- Dominant global sections have a unique simple submodule of the specified
positive highest weight. Every nonzero equivariant map from the longest-dot top
cohomology has exactly this image. The final clause is the nondominant boundary
vanishing. No comparison map is required or declared to be nonzero.

Source: Andersen, pp.55 and 58, Lemma 4; Milne, 22.17--22.30, pp.467--471.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-top-bottom-cohomology-simple-image)

Proof plan (the accepted stage-1 proof):
1. Choose a normalizer element $n$ conjugating $B$ to $B^-$ using
   [lem-sfb-torus-normalizer-borel-transitivity]. Conjugation carries the root spaces of the
   first Borel to those of the second, so its Weyl element sends $\Phi^+$ to $-\Phi^+$ and is
   $w_0$. Thus $nUn^{-1}=U^-$, and $n$ carries a $U$-fixed vector of weight $z$ to a $U^-$-fixed
   vector of weight $w_0z$. The reverse holds as $w_0^2=1$ by [lem-sfb-rho-regular-weyl-orbit].
   We will use this also on duals. Torus coactions split into weights: writing $c(v)=\sum_a
   v_a\otimes t^a$ in Laurent monomials, coassociativity and the counit give $c(v_a)=v_a\otimes
   t^a$ and $v=\sum_a v_a$. This proves that taking a specified weight space preserves
   surjections of finite-dimensional rational $T$-modules.
2. For any finite-dimensional rational $S$-module $V$, evaluation at $1$ gives an injective
   linear map $\operatorname{Hom}_S(V,H^0(z))\to\operatorname{Hom}_{B^-}(V,F_z)$. Indeed
   $a(v)=\phi(v)(1)$ satisfies $a(bv)=z(b)a(v)$, and equivariance recovers
   $\phi(v)(g)=a(g^{-1}v)$. Conversely this formula is regular by the rational action, satisfies
   $\phi(v)(gb)=z(b)^{-1}\phi(v)(g)$, and is $S$-equivariant. The function-space identification
   is exactly [lem-sfb-flag-cohomology-finite-exact]. A functional $a$ is $B^-$-linear to $F_z$
   precisely when it is a $U^-$-fixed vector of weight $-z$ in $V^\vee$. All formulas are
   identities after every scalar extension, not just on $F$-points.
3. Suppose $x$ is dominant. The dominant-character node supplies a finite-dimensional module
   with a nonzero $B$-eigenvector $v$ of weight $x$. Replace it by the rational submodule $C$
   generated by $v$. The cyclic weight bound says $C_x=Fv$. Choose a proper rational submodule
   $K$ of largest dimension. It cannot contain $v$, so $J=C/K$ is simple with a nonzero
   $U$-fixed vector of weight $x$. The simple-module node says $J^U$ is a line, hence its
   highest weight is $x$, and $J^\vee$ is simple of highest weight $-w_0x$. Step 1 gives in
   $J^\vee$ a $U^-$-fixed line of weight $-x$. A nonzero vector on it is a $B^-$-map $J\to F_x$,
   and step 2 embeds $J$ in $H^0(x)$: its kernel is a proper submodule of the simple $J$. Thus
   $H^0(x)\ne0$; it is finite-dimensional by the flag-cohomology node.
4. Apply [lem-sfb-induced-module-primitive-line] with Borel $B^-$ and character $x$. Its longest
   element is again $w_0$, so $H^0(x)^{U^-}$ is one-dimensional of weight $w_0x$. Every simple
   submodule $J'$ has a nonzero $U^-$-fixed line by [lem-sfb-simple-module-dual-highest-weight]
   applied with Borel $B^-$. That line must be this same line. Two simple submodules therefore
   have a nonzero intersection and are equal. Step 3 supplies one; call it $L(x)$. Conjugating
   its invariant line by $n^{-1}$ shows that its positive highest weight is $x$. This proves
   existence, uniqueness and the stated highest weight, without using the linkage node above
   this item. It applies equally to the dominant weight $a=-w_0x$: the map $-w_0$ permutes the
   positive coroots and hence preserves dominance.
5. Put $a=-w_0x$, $A=H^0(a)$ and $D=H^N(w_0\mathbin\cdot x)$. Since $w_0\rho=-\rho$, we have
   $w_0\mathbin\cdot x=w_0x-2\rho$ and $-(w_0\mathbin\cdot x)-2\rho=a$. The perfect invariant
   pairing of [lem-sfb-flag-line-equivariant-serre-pairing], with $i=N$, identifies $D$ with
   $A^\vee$, for the contragredient action: $\langle gd,ga'\rangle=\langle d,a'\rangle$.
   Restriction of functionals to the submodule $L(a)\subseteq A$ is an equivariant surjection
   $A^\vee\to L(a)^\vee$ (extend a basis of $L(a)$ to one of $A$). The simple-module duality
   node gives highest weight $-w_0a=x$ for $L(a)^\vee$, so it is isomorphic to $L(x)$. Composing
   this surjection and isomorphism with $L(x)\hookrightarrow H^0(x)$ gives a nonzero map $q:D\to
   H^0(x)$ with image $L(x)$.
6. For $D=A^\vee$, step 2 injects $\operatorname{Hom}_S(D,H^0(x))$ into $(D^\vee)^{U^-}_{-x}$.
   Evaluation identifies $D^\vee$ with $A$ equivariantly in finite dimension. The primitive-line
   node with Borel $B^-$ and character $a$ gives $A^{U^-}$ one-dimensional of weight $w_0a=-x$.
   Consequently the Hom space has dimension at most one. The nonzero $q$ of step 5 spans it;
   every nonzero map is $c q$ with $c\in F^\times$ and has exactly the same image $L(x)$. This
   supplies the missing argument that a unique head and a unique socle by themselves would not
   supply.
7. Finally assume $x+\rho$ is dominant while $x$ is not. Some simple coroot has $r=\langle
   x,\alpha^\vee\rangle<0$. Since $r$ is an integer and $\langle\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle=1$,
   dominance of $x+\rho$ gives $r\ge-1$, hence $r=-1$. Apply the final vanishing clause of
   [lem-sfb-minimal-parabolic-line-cohomology] with the one-dimensional trivial
   $P_\alpha$-module $V=F$ and $\mu=x$ to get $H^0(x)=0$. If the root system is empty there is
   no such nondominant $x$; the preceding image proof still works: $N=0$, $w_0=1$, and the
   pairing and invariant-line assertions explicitly allow rank zero. No comparison map is
   assumed nonzero.
-/
theorem top_bottom_cohomology_simple_image
    {F : Type u} [Field F] [IsAlgClosed F]
    (p : ℕ) [CharP F p] (hp : 0 < p)
    (S T B Bm U : Grp (Over (Spec (.of F))))
    [IsAffine S.X.left] [IsAffine T.X.left] [IsAffine U.X.left]
    (hS : IsSemisimpleGroupScheme S.X)
    (hsc : ∀ (S' : Over (Spec (.of F))) [GrpObj S'],
      IsSemisimpleGroupScheme S' → ∀ (f : S' ⟶ S.X) [IsMonHom f],
      Surjective f.left → IsFinite (GrpObj.ker f).hom →
      (∀ (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [Algebra F A]
        (z : StandardGroupSchemes.affine F A ⟶ GrpObj.ker f)
        (s : StandardGroupSchemes.affine F A ⟶ S'),
        (z ≫ GrpObj.kerι f) * s = s * (z ≫ GrpObj.kerι f)) → IsIso f)
    (iT : T ⟶ S) (iB : B ⟶ S) (j : T ⟶ B)
    (iBm : Bm ⟶ S) (jm : T ⟶ Bm)
    (hB : IsRhoBorelPair iT iB j) (hBm : IsRhoBorelPair iT iBm jm)
    [IsCommMonObj T.X] [IsCommMonObj (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)]
    (aS : ModObj T.X S.X) (aB : ModObj T.X B.X) (aBm : ModObj T.X Bm.X)
    (hconjS : aS.smul = (iT.hom.hom ⊗ₘ 𝟙 S.X) ≫ GrpObj.conj S.X)
    (hconjB : aB.smul = (j.hom.hom ⊗ₘ 𝟙 B.X) ≫ GrpObj.conj B.X)
    (hconjBm : aBm.smul = (jm.hom.hom ⊗ₘ 𝟙 Bm.X) ≫ GrpObj.conj Bm.X)
    (Φ : Finset (Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F))))
    (P : RootDatum Φ
      (Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)))
      (Additive (Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F) ⟶ T)))
    (b : P.Base) [P.IsReduced]
    (hroot : ∀ α : Φ, P.root α = α.val)
    (hpair : ∀ (χ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)))
      (ν : Additive (Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F) ⟶ T)),
      ν.toMul.hom.hom ≫ χ.toMul.hom.hom =
        (𝟙 (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)) ^ (P.toLinearMap χ ν))
    (hweights : ∀ χ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)),
      χ ∈ Φ ↔ χ ≠ 0 ∧ @TorusCharacterTangent.weightSpace F _ T S aS χ.toMul ≠ ⊥)
    (hpos : ∀ α : Φ, b.IsPos α ↔
      @TorusCharacterTangent.weightSpace F _ T B aB α.val.toMul ≠ ⊥)
    (hopp : ∀ χ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)),
      (χ ≠ 0 ∧ @TorusCharacterTangent.weightSpace F _ T Bm aBm χ.toMul ≠ ⊥) ↔
        ∃ α : Φ, b.IsPos α ∧ χ = -P.root α)
    (ρ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)))
    (hρ : (2 : ℤ) • ρ =
      ∑ α ∈ (@Finset.filter Φ b.IsPos (Classical.decPred _) Finset.univ), P.root α)
    (n : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ S.X) (e : T ≅ T)
    (he : e.hom.hom.hom ≫ iT.hom.hom =
      (toUnit T.X ≫ n⁻¹) * iT.hom.hom * (toUnit T.X ≫ n))
    (hlongest :
      (fun α : Φ ↦ Additive.ofMul (e.hom ≫ α.val.toMul)) '' {α : Φ | b.IsPos α} =
        (fun α : Φ ↦ -α.val) '' {α : Φ | b.IsPos α})
    (iU : U ⟶ B)
    (hUclosed : IsClosedSubgroupScheme iU.hom.hom)
    (hUnormal : IsNormalSubgroupScheme iU.hom.hom)
    (hUsmooth : Smooth U.X.hom) (hUconnected : ConnectedSpace U.X.left)
    (hUunipotent : SolvableGroupStructure.IsUnipotent U)
    (hUmax : ∀ (V : Grp (Over (Spec (.of F)))) (iV : V ⟶ B),
      IsClosedSubgroupScheme iV.hom.hom → IsNormalSubgroupScheme iV.hom.hom →
      Smooth V.X.hom → ConnectedSpace V.X.left →
      SolvableGroupStructure.IsUnipotent V → ∃ f : V ⟶ U, f ≫ iU = iV)
    (pm : Bm ⟶ T) (hpm : jm ≫ pm = 𝟙 T)
    (hpmU : ∀ (V : Grp (Over (Spec (.of F)))) (iV : V ⟶ Bm),
      IsClosedSubgroupScheme iV.hom.hom → IsNormalSubgroupScheme iV.hom.hom →
      Smooth V.X.hom → ConnectedSpace V.X.left →
      SolvableGroupStructure.IsUnipotent V → iV ≫ pm = 1)
    (Y : Over (Spec (.of F))) (π : S.X ⟶ Y)
    (hY : IsProper Y.hom) (hYft : LocallyOfFiniteType Y.hom)
    (hYqc : QuasiCompact Y.hom) (hπf : Flat π.left) (hπs : Surjective π.left)
    (hπi : (fst S.X Bm.X * (snd S.X Bm.X ≫ iBm.hom.hom)) ≫ π = fst S.X Bm.X ≫ π)
    (hπt : IsPullback (fst S.X Bm.X)
      (fst S.X Bm.X * (snd S.X Bm.X ≫ iBm.hom.hom)) π π)
    (D : ∀ χ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)),
      CharacterLineCohomology.Data S Bm iBm Y π (pm ≫ χ.toMul)) :
    (∀ x : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)),
      (∀ α ∈ b.support, 0 ≤ P.toLinearMap x (P.coroot α)) →
      Nontrivial (cohomology Y (D x).bundle.L 0) ∧
      ∃ L : Submodule F (cohomology Y (D x).bundle.L 0),
        IsStable ((D x).coaction 0) L ∧ L ≠ ⊥ ∧
        (∀ K : Submodule F (cohomology Y (D x).bundle.L 0),
          IsStable ((D x).coaction 0) K → K ≤ L → K = ⊥ ∨ K = L) ∧
        (∀ K : Submodule F (cohomology Y (D x).bundle.L 0),
          IsStable ((D x).coaction 0) K → K ≠ ⊥ →
          (∀ J : Submodule F (cohomology Y (D x).bundle.L 0),
            IsStable ((D x).coaction 0) J → J ≤ K → J = ⊥ ∨ J = K) → K = L) ∧
        Module.finrank F ↥(L ⊓ invariants ((D x).coaction 0) (iU ≫ iB)) = 1 ∧
        (∀ v ∈ (L ⊓ invariants ((D x).coaction 0) (iU ≫ iB)),
          (coordinateMap iT).toLinearMap.lTensor (cohomology Y (D x).bundle.L 0)
              ((D x).coaction 0 v) = v ⊗ₜ[F] characterElement x.toMul) ∧
        ∀ f : cohomology Y
            (D (Additive.ofMul (e.hom ≫ (x + ρ).toMul) - ρ)).bundle.L
            (@Finset.filter Φ b.IsPos (Classical.decPred _) Finset.univ).card →ₗ[F]
            cohomology Y (D x).bundle.L 0,
          f ≠ 0 →
          f.rTensor (coordinateAlgebra S) ∘ₗ
              (D (Additive.ofMul (e.hom ≫ (x + ρ).toMul) - ρ)).coaction
                (@Finset.filter Φ b.IsPos (Classical.decPred _) Finset.univ).card =
            (D x).coaction 0 ∘ₗ f → LinearMap.range f = L) ∧
    (∀ x : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)),
      (∀ α ∈ b.support, 0 ≤ P.toLinearMap (x + ρ) (P.coroot α)) →
      ¬ (∀ α ∈ b.support, 0 ≤ P.toLinearMap x (P.coroot α)) →
      Subsingleton (cohomology Y (D x).bundle.L 0)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.RationalHighestWeight
